passages.text:
Names
{{stack|}}
The most likely etymology of the primary common name for the fruit, the kaffir lime, is through the Kaffirs, an ethnic group in Sri Lanka partly descended from enslaved Bantu.

 {{lang|jv|jêruk purut}} in Javanese and {{lang|ms|limau purut}} in Malay (respectively into Indonesian and Malaysian) both meaning "warty/rough-skinned lime" due to the fruit's bumpy texture.
 {{transliteration|zh|jiàn yè chéng}} ({{lang|zh|箭叶橙}} "arrow-leaf lime") in Chinese.{{fact|date=November 2025}}
 {{lang|tl|kabuyaw}} or {{lang|tl|kulubot}} in the Philippines. The city of Cabuyao in Laguna is named after the fruit.
Kolumichai, கொலுமிச்சை in Kongu Tamil.
 {{transliteration|th|makrud}} or {{transliteration|th|makrut}} ({{lang|th|[[wikt:มะกรูด|มะกรูด]]}}, {{IPA|lang=th|/máʔ.krùːt/}}) in Thailand (a name also used for the bergamot orange).
 {{transliteration|lo|mak khi hut}} ({{lang|lo|ໝາກຂີ້ຫູດ}}, {{IPA|lang=lo|/ma᷆ːk.kʰi᷆ː.hu᷆ːt/}}) in Laos.{{fact|date=November 2025}}
 {{lang|vi|chúc}} or {{lang|vi|chanh Thái}} in Vietnam.
 kroch saech (ក្រូចសើច) in Cambodia
 {{lang|fr|combava}} in Réunion Island.{{fact|date=November 2025}}
